      "retry": {
        "max_attempts": 2,
        "repair_attempts": 1,
        "retry_instruction": "Return only JSON matching the declared schema. Preserve all supplied claim IDs in order and keep each of the three independent assessments internally consistent.",
        "terminal_failure": "record_not_applicable_failure"
      },
      "seed": 42,
      "system_prompt": "Act as a strict closed-world entailment verifier. For every atomic claim, independently first determine which full-KB candidate-rule antecedents apply, then independently assess full-KB entailment, exact-trace entailment, and common-reference item-fact support. Do not derive one field from another. Supported requires semantic entailment; unsupported means relevant evidence does not entail the claim; contradicted requires affirmative conflict; not_verifiable means the supplied evidence cannot settle the claim. Absence of support is not contradiction. Preserve every supplied claim ID exactly once and do not introduce claims, rules, sources, or knowledge from outside the packet. SUPPORTED requires the supplied source directly to entail the complete claim; CONTRADICTED requires direct incompatible or negated evidence; UNSUPPORTED applies only when relevant supplied material is within scope but does not entail; NOT_VERIFIABLE is outside the supplied source's evaluable scope. A rule is usable only when its antecedent is established and its consequent directly entails the claim: do not use category analogy, or treat an example list's omissions as negative evidence. Common-reference support is only for concrete supplied item/query/context facts, never styling principles, suitability, or subjective rationale. Mark common_reference_eligible accordingly. Return exactly one complete judgment for every supplied claim; an empty claims array is invalid whenever atomic claims are supplied.",
      "temperature": 0.0,
      "template_variables": [
        "full_kb_rules_json",
        "exact_trace_rules_json",
        "common_reference_facts_json",
        "explanation",
        "claims_json"
      ],
